# Delegation of Authority Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## Article I — Purpose

{{ org_name }} ("the Organization") manages multiple simultaneous STTR partnerships, federal grants, and cooperative agreements. This policy establishes clear authority levels for approvals, signatures, and commitments so that day-to-day operations proceed without unnecessary bottlenecks while maintaining the internal controls required by 2 CFR 200.303 and the Organization's fiduciary obligations.

No individual should have to guess whether they have the authority to act. This policy answers that question definitively.

## Article II — Scope

This policy applies to all directors, officers, employees, principal investigators, and agents of the Organization who are authorized to obligate organizational funds, sign agreements, or make commitments on behalf of the Organization.

## Article III — Designated Officers

For purposes of this policy, the following roles carry delegated authority:

| Role | Current Holder | Appointment Basis |
|------|---------------|-------------------|
| Board Chair | [current Board Chair] | [board action establishing this role] |
| Executive Director (Vice-Chair) | [current Executive Director] | [board action establishing this role] |
| Fiscal Custodian | [current fiscal custodian] | [board action establishing this role] |

**Principal Investigator (PI)** refers to the individual designated as PI on a specific federal award or proposal. PI authority under this policy is limited to the award(s) for which they are named.

## Article IV — Authority Matrix

### 4.1 Grant and Contract Proposals

| Action | Authority | Signature |
|--------|-----------|-----------|
| Proposals under $1,000,000 | Executive Director approves and signs | Executive Director |
| Proposals $1,000,000–$25,000,000 | Executive Director approves; Board Chair co-signs | Executive Director + Board Chair |
| Proposals over $25,000,000 | Board of Directors approval required | Board Chair (after board vote) |

### 4.2 Teaming Agreements and Partnerships

| Action | Authority |
|--------|-----------|
| Teaming agreements under $1,000,000 | Executive Director |
| Teaming agreements $1,000,000 or above | Board of Directors approval |
| Any teaming agreement involving export-controlled work (ITAR/EAR) | Board of Directors approval |

Export-controlled teaming agreements require board approval regardless of dollar value. See the Organization's Export Control Policy for additional compliance requirements.

### 4.3 Purchase Orders and Contracts

| Amount | Required Approval |
|--------|-------------------|
| Up to $10,000 (micro-purchase) | Principal Investigator of the relevant award |
| $10,001–$100,000 | Executive Director |
| Over $100,000 | Executive Director + Board Chair co-approval |

All purchases must also comply with the Organization's Procurement Policy, including competition requirements and vendor verification.

### 4.4 Hiring and Contractor Agreements

| Action | Authority |
|--------|-----------|
| Hiring or contractor agreements under $100,000/year | Executive Director |
| Hiring or contractor agreements $100,000/year or above | Board of Directors approval |

### 4.5 Travel Authorization

| Action | Authority |
|--------|-----------|
| Domestic travel | PI self-certifies per the Travel Policy |
| International travel | Executive Director approval required |

See the Organization's Travel Policy for reimbursement procedures and rate limits.

### 4.6 Award Administration

| Action | Authority |
|--------|-----------|
| No-cost extensions | Executive Director |
| Budget modifications under 10% of total award value | Executive Director |
| Budget modifications 10% or above of total award value | Executive Director, with Board Chair notification |
| Subaward issuance | Executive Director (in compliance with Procurement Policy and Subaward Monitoring Policy) |

### 4.7 Communications and Legal

| Action | Authority |
|--------|-----------|
| Press releases and public statements | Executive Director |
| Legal filings, lawsuits, or settlement agreements | Board of Directors approval required |

## Article V — Succession of Authority

If the Executive Director is unavailable (due to incapacity, conflict of interest, travel, or any other reason), the Board Chair assumes all Executive Director authorities under this policy.

If both the Executive Director and Board Chair are unavailable, authority passes to the next available officer of the Organization as designated by the Board of Directors. The Board shall maintain a current succession list and communicate it to all officers.

The unavailability of a designee does not suspend the Organization's ability to act. The succession chain ensures continuous operational authority at all times.

## Article VI — Emergency Authority

When a time-sensitive situation arises — including but not limited to proposal deadlines, emergency procurements, safety incidents, or regulatory filing deadlines — and the normally required approver is unavailable, the available senior officer (in the succession order defined in Article V) may act with full authority for the transaction at hand.

**Conditions for emergency authority:**

1. The delay required to obtain normal approval would cause material harm to the Organization (missed deadline, safety risk, regulatory penalty, or loss of funding opportunity);
2. A reasonable, good-faith effort has been made to reach the normally required approver;
3. The acting officer documents the emergency action in writing, including the nature of the emergency and efforts made to contact the normal approver; and
4. The Board of Directors is notified in writing within **five (5) business days** of the emergency action.

Emergency authority does not override the conflict-of-interest prohibition in Article VII.

## Article VII — Prohibited Actions

### 7.1 Self-Dealing Prohibition

No individual may approve their own compensation, reimbursement, bonus, or any transaction in which they have a direct or indirect financial interest. Such approvals must be made by a disinterested party at an equal or higher authority level:

- Executive Director compensation and reimbursements: Board Chair approves.
- Board Chair compensation and reimbursements: Board of Directors approves (with the Chair recused).
- PI reimbursements: Executive Director approves.

### 7.2 Splitting Prohibition

Splitting a transaction into smaller parts to circumvent the authority thresholds in this policy is prohibited. Authority levels are determined by the total expected value of the commitment, not individual invoices or phases.

### 7.3 Retroactive Approval

Except as provided under the emergency authority provisions of Article VI, retroactive approval of commitments is prohibited. An individual who commits organizational funds without proper authority may be held personally responsible and is subject to disciplinary action.

## Article VIII — Record Keeping

### 8.1 Documentation Requirement

All approvals under this policy must be documented in writing. Acceptable forms of written approval include:

- Signed paper documents;
- Email from the approver's organizational email account;
- Electronic approval through the Organization's designated systems (e.g., Focusmail); and
- Recorded board votes in meeting minutes.

### 8.2 Retention

Approval records shall be retained in accordance with the Organization's Document Retention Policy and applicable federal requirements (2 CFR 200.334), whichever period is longer.

### 8.3 Audit Trail

The Fiscal Custodian shall maintain a log of all commitments exceeding $10,000, including the approver, date, amount, and purpose. This log shall be available to the Board of Directors upon request and reviewed as part of the annual audit.

## Article IX — Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy at least annually. The review shall consider whether the authority thresholds remain appropriate given the Organization's growth, the volume and size of active awards, and any changes in federal requirements. Revisions require a majority vote of the Board and shall be documented in the meeting minutes.

The Executive Director shall report to the Board annually on any instances in which emergency authority was exercised and any instances of noncompliance with this policy.
